NAME
       devctl - ask the device manager (devd) about disks, drives and discs

SYNOPSIS
       devctl list
       devctl monitor
       devctl fstypes
       devctl mount [--recover] name
       devctl unmount name
       devctl eject disk
       devctl format name fs [--label label] [--full] [--cluster bytes]
       devctl cancel name

DESCRIPTION
       devctl talks to devd(1), for testing it without a desktop.

       list      the disks and volumes: size, file system, class (usb, cd
                 for a CD/DVD drive, int for a built-in disk), whether it is
                 read-only, whether it belongs to the running system,
                 whether devd can mount it, its label and where it is
                 mounted.

       monitor   print every device event (ADDED, REMOVED, CHANGED) until
                 interrupted.

       fstypes   the file systems devd knows, which it can mount and which
                 it can format.

       mount, unmount
                 mount a volume (name as in devctl list, e.g. sdb1) at
                 /media/<user>/<label> and print the path; unmount it.
                 An ext4 or NTFS volume whose journal still holds changes
                 (the drive was pulled out, or the computer turned off,
                 before they were written) is not mounted: devctl says so
                 (EUCLEAN) and names --recover, which replays the journal
                 -- writing those changes to the drive -- and then mounts
                 it.

       eject     unmount every volume of a drive (e.g. sdb) and flush it, so
                 it can be pulled out; a CD/DVD drive (e.g. sr0) opens its
                 tray.

       format    make a new file system (vfat, ext4, exfat or ntfs) on a
                 volume.  Quick unless --full is given, which writes zeros
                 over the whole volume first.  --cluster sets the allocation
                 unit size, one of those devctl fstypes lists for the file
                 system.  Everything on the volume is lost.

                 Ctrl-C (SIGINT) or SIGTERM stops the format: devctl asks
                 devd to cancel it, waits until it has stopped, prints
                 devd's message and exits with 1.  When nothing had been
                 written yet the volume is unchanged; otherwise devd clears
                 its first and last megabyte, and the volume must be
                 formatted before it can be used.  If the format finished
                 just before, its result is printed instead.  When the
                 cancel is refused, the next Ctrl-C ends devctl and the
                 format goes on.

       cancel    stop the format of a volume that another devctl or the
                 file manager started, the same way.  Allowed for root and
                 for the user who started it, while logged in at the
                 console.  Fails with ENOENT when no format of the volume
                 is running.

       mount, unmount, eject and format are allowed for root and for the
       user logged in at the console: on USB drives all four (format not
       when write-protected), on CD/DVD drives all but format, on the
       partitions of built-in disks mount and unmount only -- never on a
       disk the running system uses.

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success, 1 when devd refused or failed (the reason is printed),
       2 for a bad argument.

SEE ALSO
       devd(1), lsblk(1), mount(1)

